  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Integrated management of Jaguar Habitat through Community Engagement in Western Mexico</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to support jaguar conservation efforts in local communities and private lands from the Mexican states of Jalisco, Nayarit and Durango, enrolled in the Biodiversity Endowment Fund. This project is intended to conserve jaguars (Panthera onca) through environmental outreach and technical training for communities and landowners that will heighten awareness of the ecological and socioeconomic importance of the feline, reduce illegal hunting of jaguars and their prey, mitigate conflicts between livestock and big cats, and avoid further fragmentation of jaguars habitat. Specific activities include: (1) identification of priorities and challenges to jaguar conservation in each community; (2) determine conservation opportunities and challenges through participatory diagnostics; (3) sensitizing landowners and communities on the ecological and socioeconomic importance of jaguars and their prey; (4) training community brigades in the use of camera traps and GPS devices to monitor jaguars and their prey; (5) training land owners on best management practices to avoid feline conflicts and restore habitat; (6) and providing a space for beneficiaries to share experiences with economic alternatives that respect the felines habitat and foment its survival.</narrative>
  </description>
